b.add(S8, 325, "management", "What primary management is printed for all patients with heart failure?", "ARNI plus a beta blocker", ["Nesiritide plus digoxin", "Hydralazine plus nitrate", "Verapamil plus diltiazem"], "The line reads for all patients with HF: ARNI + β blocker.")
b.add(S8, 325, "recall", "What hierarchy follows in the next note?", "ARNI > beta blocker > ACE inhibitor > ARB", ["Beta blocker > ARNI > ARB > ACE inhibitor", "ACE inhibitor > ARB > ARNI > beta blocker", "Diuretic > digoxin > verapamil > nesiritide"], "The hierarchy line is ARNI > β blocker > ACE inhibitor > ARB.")
b.add(S8, 325, "scenario", "A congested HF patient is not yet euvolemic. What sequence does the page print before starting a beta blocker?", "Give diuretics first to relieve congestion, then start the beta blocker", ["Start beta blocker immediately; diuretics can wait", "Start verapamil and diltiazem together", "Perform cardiac transplantation before diuresis"], "The note says if not euvolemic, diuretics first to relieve congestion and only then start beta blocker, otherwise pulmonary edema can occur.")
b.add(S8, 325, "fillup", "Heart failure with renal failure is paired on page 325 with ______ plus nitrate.", "hydralazine", ["finerenone", "sacubitril", "dobutamine"], "The line reads heart failure with renal failure: hydralazine + nitrate.")
b.add(S8, 325, "match", "Match the Fantastic Four to their printed drug/class detail — 1) First 2) Second 3) Third 4) Fourth … A) Aldosterone antagonist finerenone (non-steroidal) B) Beta blocker C) ARNI D) SGLT2 inhibitors such as empagliflozin and dapagliflozin", "1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D", ["1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-B, 4-D", "1-C, 2-B, 3-D, 4-A"], "The list is ARNI, beta blocker, aldosterone antagonist finerenone and SGLT2 inhibitors empagliflozin/dapagliflozin.")
b.add(S8, 325, "numeric", "In the refractory-HF pathway, above which heart rate is ivabradine added to maximum-tolerated beta blocker plus Fantastic Four?", "70/min", ["50/min", "90/min", "120/min"], "The arrow reads if HR >70/min, add ivabradine, a funny-current inhibitor with visual disturbances.")
b.add(S8, 325, "match", "Match each refractory-HF branch to its treatment — 1) Wide QRS 2) Normal QRS 3) Persistent symptoms after the wide-QRS therapy … A) LV assist devices or implantable cardioverter defibrillator B) Cardiac resynchronization therapy C) Cardiac transplant", "1-B, 2-A, 3-C", ["1-A, 2-B, 3-C", "1-B, 2-C, 3-A", "1-C, 2-B, 3-A"], "With wide QRS the page moves CRT → transplant, while the normal-QRS branch goes to LV assisted devices or ICD.")

b.guide(S1, "The ACC/AHA 2017 definition calls heart failure a complex clinical syndrome from structural/functional cardiac disease that impairs ventricular filling or ejection; structural LVH and functional AF are the examples, and the printed ACC/AHA expansions are retained (p318).\nThe four types are HFrEF EF <40%, HFpEF/HFNEF EF ≥50%, HFmrEF EF 40–50% and ADHF. The NYHA first-line symptoms are angina, dyspnea and palpitation; syncope is explicitly excluded.\nNYHA I–IV progress from symptoms with more than ordinary activity, ordinary activity, less than ordinary activity to rest; the mortality column gives negligible mortality, sudden cardiac death across II–III and LV-failure symptoms in IV.")
b.guide(S2, "Stage A has risk factors (HTN, atherosclerosis, DM, OSA) without symptoms or structural disease; stage B has structural disease without symptoms (HTN → LVH, previous MI → remodelling); stage C is the commonest and symptomatic; stage D is refractory to medical therapy (p319).\nThe most common overall presentation is HFrEF, NYHA 2/3, stage C.\nSystolic LV-forward failure from reduced cardiac output lists fatigue with cachexia/TNF-α, decreased urine output, cool extremities, cyanosis, altered mentation, S3, cardiomegaly/dilated LV, displaced apex, pulsus alternans and narrow pulse pressure.")
b.guide(S3, "Concentric LVH reducing cavity size and systolic failure with insufficient dilation/increased ESV both raise LVEDP; the chain continues through LAP and PCVP to pulmonary exudation (p320).\nIts consequences are dyspnea (predominant symptom), acute crackles and predominantly right-sided pleural effusion with right-side lying preference. Orthopnea is immediate on lying down; PND occurs after 2–3 hours.\nPND follows supine posture → increased interstitial-to-intravascular fluid shift → RA → pulmonary circulation → failure to accommodate the load → air hunger.")
b.guide(S4, "Right-heart failure is most often caused by left-heart failure through raised RVEDP, then raised RA and venacaval pressures produce impending AJR, ascites, edema, hepatomegaly and raised JVP; AJR is palpated periumbilically (p321).\nCauses are DCMP (genetic cause most common), ischemic cardiomyopathy post-MI/CAD, valvular disease (severe AR, very severe AS or severe MR) and congenital heart disease.\nFramingham major criteria are acute pulmonary edema, cardiomegaly, AJR, raised JVP, PND/orthopnea, crepitations/rales and S3 gallop; minor criteria are ankle edema, exertional dyspnea, hepatomegaly, nocturnal cough, pleural effusion and tachycardia >120 bpm.")
b.guide(S5, "HFpEF/HFNEF begins with comorbidity-driven microvascular inflammation → LV dysfunction → longitudinal-fibre/untwisting dysfunction → LV diastolic dysfunction (p322). Compensatory LVH reduces cavity size and raises LVEDP and LAP.\nOne arm raises PCWP to produce interstitial/alveolar edema and dyspnea; the other stretches/dilates the atria, raises their CO contribution >40% and predisposes to AF. The film caption shows bilateral pulmonary edema and pleural effusions.\nHF has position-changing gravitational crepitations, cold peripheries and dyspnea > cough; COPD has non-positional fine crepitations, warm CO₂-retaining peripheries and cough > dyspnea. Bendopnea is advanced HF with 30 seconds of bending; trepopnea is breathlessness on one side (right-side preference with right effusion); platypnea is named without definition.")
b.guide(S6, "The ESC HFNEF definition needs CHF symptoms, normal/mildly abnormal LV systolic function and abnormal LV relaxation; typical patients are older women with HTN, obesity, CKD, anemia, diabetes or CAD and more AF than HFrEF (p323).\nADHF risk factors are non-compliance, LRTI/IE, anemia from CAD aspirin → GI bleed, renal failure, pregnancy, uncontrolled HTN, myocardial ischemia, NSAIDs (naproxen printed as safest in HF), LV-depressing verapamil/diltiazem, and arrhythmias—especially AF.\nHFrEF pathogenesis runs index event (DCM/ischemia) → HFrEF → compensation → remodelling to maintain CO; sympathetic activation raises contractility and HR but predisposes to arrhythmias.")
b.guide(S7, "RAAS compensation raises afterload through angiotensin II and preload through aldosterone-mediated salt/water retention; ADH retains free water and causes hyponatremia, while natriuretic peptides inhibit RAAS/sympathetic activity and excrete salt and water (p324).\nSustained activation of mechanisms 1–3 causes secondary end-organ damage, worsens LV remodelling and leads to decompensation. Nesiritide is printed as no longer used; treatment blocks sympathetic activity, RAAS and degradation of natriuretic peptides with a neprilysin inhibitor.\nBeta blockers include metoprolol, bisoprolol (most preferred, 1.25 mg) and carvedilol (3.125 mg BD). ACE inhibitors/ARBs have a class effect, with ramipril/perindopril named and ACE > ARB because ACE raises bradykinin.")
b.guide(S8, "ARNI combines valsartan (ARB) and sacubitril (NI), with the printed diagram wording preserved as a source caveat (p325). Natriuretic peptides listed are ANP, BNP, substance P, adrenomedulline and bradykinin.\nPrimary management for all HF is ARNI + beta blocker, ranked ARNI > beta blocker > ACE inhibitor > ARB; if the patient is not euvolemic, diuretics must come first to avoid pulmonary edema. Renal-failure HF is paired with hydralazine + nitrate.\nThe Fantastic Four are ARNI, beta blocker, non-steroidal aldosterone antagonist finerenone and SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin/dapagliflozin). If refractory and HR >70/min, ivabradine (funny-current inhibitor; visual disturbances) is added; wide-QRS refractory HF goes to CRT then transplant, while normal QRS goes to LV assist devices or ICD.")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    b.write()
